--TEST--
A standard output that is not a terminal reports no colour at all, so a piped run and a CI log are plain — the agreement between `isTty` and `colorDepth` that `rule:tooling/the-terminal-profile-resolves-once` states
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Cli\Stream
//
// § 3: "When the stream is not a terminal, styling is dropped entirely", so
// `myprog | grep` and a CI log are plain. That makes these two members answer
// one question between them, and this case asserts they agree rather than
// asserting what either one said — the whole point being that a build which
// grew its own colour heuristic fails here while still looking right on its
// own line.
//
// A conformance case's own output is captured, so none of the three streams is
// a terminal while this runs.

bool $in = Core\Cli::isTty(Core\Cli\Stream::In);
bool $out = Core\Cli::isTty(Core\Cli\Stream::Out);
bool $err = Core\Cli::isTty(Core\Cli\Stream::Err);

// The implication, one direction only: colour requires a terminal, but a
// terminal does not require colour (`NO_COLOR` and `TERM=dumb` both refuse).
if ($out) {
    echo "held (a terminal may or may not colour)", "\n";
} else {
    if (Core\Cli::colorDepth() == Core\Cli\ColorDepth::None) {
        echo "held (no terminal, no colour)", "\n";
    } else {
        echo "broken: colour without a terminal", "\n";
    }
}

// Each stream answers separately, which is why `isTty` takes one rather than
// the process answering once.
if ($in) { echo "in "; } else { echo "in:no "; }
if ($out) { echo "out "; } else { echo "out:no "; }
if ($err) { echo "err", "\n"; } else { echo "err:no", "\n"; }

// The dimensions are answered whether or not there is a terminal to measure:
// a program that lays out a table must not have to branch on tty-ness first.
if (Core\Cli::width() >= 1) {
    if (Core\Cli::height() >= 1) {
        echo "measured either way", "\n";
    }
}
--EXPECT--
held (no terminal, no colour)
in:no out:no err:no
measured either way
